  "summary": "New sequencing surveys expand known viral diversity in kelp, mesopelagic seawater and CRESS DNA viruses, including algal and fungal endogenous elements.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is sequence-based discovery of viral diversity in algal and marine systems. An RNA virome of New Zealand bull kelp reveals novel viral lineages, with higher diversity at a city-proximal site and in unhealthy kelp. Long-read metagenomics of mesopelagic Andaman Sea seawater yields 1,604 high-confidence viral contigs, mostly bacteriophages, with genomic novelty. A phylogenetic survey of CRESS DNA viruses combines environmental metagenomes, reference genomes and fungal and algal endogenous viral elements, revealing unclassified lineages. What is new is the combination of long-read and host-associated or genome-embedded sources to resolve previously unexplored viral clades. A dengue genomic epidemiology study from Ivory Coast is a distinct, host-specific outlier within the group and does not share this thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
